[to_json()](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#function::std::to_json)Returns a JSON value parsed from the given string.
[to_str()](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/string#function::std::to_str)Render JSON value to a string.
[json_get()](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#function::std::json_get)Returns a value from a JSON object or array given its path.
[json_set()](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#function::std::json_set)Returns an updated JSON target with a new value.
[json_array_unpack()](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#function::std::json_array_unpack)Returns the elements of a JSON array as a set of json.
[json_object_pack()](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#function::std::json_object_pack)Returns the given set of key/value tuples as a JSON object.
[json_object_unpack()](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#function::std::json_object_unpack)Returns the data in a JSON object as a set of key/value tuples.
[json_typeof()](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#function::std::json_typeof)Returns the type of the outermost JSON value as a string.

JSON in Gel is a [scalar type](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib#ref-datamodel-scalar-types). This type doesn't have its own literal, and instead can be obtained by either casting a value to the [`json`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#type::std::json) type, or by using the [`to_json()`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#function::std::to_json) function:

Copy

db> select to_json('{"hello": "world"}');

{Json("{\"hello\": \"world\"}")}

Copy

db> select <json>'hello world';

{Json("\"hello world\"")}

Any value in Gel can be cast to a [`json`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#type::std::json) type as well:

Copy

db> select <json>2019;

{Json("2019")}

Copy

db> select <json>cal::to_local_date(datetime_current(), 'UTC');

{Json("\"2022-11-21\"")}

The [`json_object_pack()`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#function::std::json_object_pack) function provides one more way to construct JSON. It constructs a JSON object from an array of key/value tuples:

Copy

db> select json_object_pack({("hello", <json>"world")});

{Json("{\"hello\": \"world\"}")}

Additionally, any [`Object`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/objects#type::std::Object) in Gel can be cast as a [`json`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#type::std::json) type. This produces the same JSON value as the JSON-serialized result of that said object. Furthermore, this result will be the same as the output of a [`select expression`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/reference/edgeql/select#statement::select) in _JSON mode_, including the shape of that type:

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... 
... 
... select <json>( select schema::Object { name, timestamp := cal::to_local_date( datetime_current(), 'UTC') } filter .name = 'std::bool');

{Json("{\"name\": \"std::bool\", \"timestamp\": \"2022-11-21\"}")}

JSON values can also be cast back into scalars. Casting JSON is symmetrical meaning that, if a scalar value can be cast into JSON, a compatible JSON value can be cast into a scalar of that type. Some scalar types will have specific conditions for casting:

*   JSON strings can be cast to a [`str`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/string#type::std::str) type. Casting [`uuid`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/uuid#type::std::uuid) and [date/time](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/datetime#ref-std-datetime) types to JSON results in a JSON string representing its original value. This means it is also possible to cast a JSON string back to those types. The value of the UUID or datetime string must be properly formatted to successfully cast from JSON, otherwise Gel will raise an exception.

*   JSON numbers can be cast to any [numeric type](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/numbers#ref-std-numeric).

*   JSON booleans can be cast to a [`bool`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/bool#type::std::bool) type.

*   JSON `null` is unique because it can be cast to an empty set (`{}`) of any type.

*   JSON arrays can be cast to any valid array type, as long as the JSON array is homogeneous, does not contain `null` as an element of the array, and does not contain another array.

A named [`tuple`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/tuple#type::std::tuple) is converted into a JSON object when cast as a [`json`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#type::std::json) while a standard [`tuple`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/tuple#type::std::tuple) is converted into a JSON array.

json

Arbitrary JSON data.

Any other type can be [`cast`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/type#operator::cast) to and from JSON:

Copy

db> select <json>42;

{Json("42")}

Copy

db> select <bool>to_json('true');

{true}

A [`json`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#type::std::json) value can also be cast as a [`str`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/string#type::std::str) type, but only when recognized as a JSON string:

Copy

db> select <str>to_json('"something"');

{'something'}

Casting a JSON array of strings (`["a", "b", "c"]`) to a [`str`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/string#type::std::str) will result in an error:

Copy

db> select <str>to_json('["a", "b", "c"]');

InvalidValueError: expected json string or null; got JSON array

Instead, use the [`to_str()`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/string#function::std::to_str) function to dump a JSON value to a [`str`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/string#type::std::str) value. Use the [`to_json()`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#function::std::to_json) function to parse a JSON string to a [`json`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#type::std::json) value:

Copy

db> select to_json('[1, "a"]');

{Json("[1, \"a\"]")}

Copy

db> select to_str(<json>[1, 2]);

{'[1, 2]'}

This type is backed by the Postgres `jsonb` type which has a size limit of 256MiB minus one byte. The Gel `json` type is also subject to this limitation.

json[int64]->json

Accesses the element of the JSON string or array at a given index.

The contents of JSON _arrays_ and _strings_ can also be accessed via `[]`:

Copy

db> select <json>'hello'[1];

{Json("\"e\"")}

Copy

db> select <json>'hello'[-1];

{Json("\"o\"")}

Copy

db> select to_json('[1, "a", null]')[1];

{Json("\"a\"")}

Copy

db> select to_json('[1, "a", null]')[-1];

{Json("null")}

This will raise an exception if the specified index is not valid for the base JSON value. To access an index that is potentially out of bounds, use [`json_get()`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#function::std::json_get).

json[int64:int64]->json

Produces a JSON value comprising a portion of the existing JSON value.

JSON _arrays_ and _strings_ can be sliced in the same way as regular arrays, producing a new JSON array or string:

Copy

db> select <json>'hello'[0:2];

{Json("\"he\"")}

Copy

db> select <json>'hello'[2:];

{Json("\"llo\"")}

Copy

db> select to_json('[1, 2, 3]')[0:2];

{Json("[1, 2]")}

Copy

db> select to_json('[1, 2, 3]')[2:];

{Json("[3]")}

Copy

db> select to_json('[1, 2, 3]')[:1];

{Json("[1]")}

Copy

db> select to_json('[1, 2, 3]')[:-2];

{Json("[1]")}

json++json->json

Concatenates two JSON arrays, objects, or strings into one.

JSON arrays, objects and strings can be concatenated with JSON values of the same type into a new JSON value.

If you concatenate two JSON objects, you get a new object whose keys will be a union of the keys of the input objects. If a key is present in both objects, the value from the second object is taken.

Copy

db> select to_json('[1, 2]') ++ to_json('[3]');

{Json("[1, 2, 3]")}

Copy

db> select to_json('{"a": 1}') ++ to_json('{"b": 2}');

{Json("{\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}")}

Copy

db> select to_json('{"a": 1, "b": 2}') ++ to_json('{"b": 3}');

{Json("{\"a\": 1, \"b\": 3}")}

Copy

db> select to_json('"123"') ++ to_json('"456"');

{Json("\"123456\"")}

json[str]->json

Accesses an element of a JSON object given its key.

The fields of JSON _objects_ can also be accessed via `[]`:

Copy

db> select to_json('{"a": 2, "b": 5}')['b'];

{Json("5")}

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... select j := <json>(schema::Type { name, timestamp := cal::to_local_date(datetime_current(), 'UTC') }) filter j['name'] = <json>'std::bool';

{Json("{\"name\": \"std::bool\", \"timestamp\": \"2022-11-21\"}")}

This will raise an exception if the specified field does not exist for the base JSON value. To access an index that is potentially out of bounds, use [`json_get()`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#function::std::json_get).

std::to_json(string: str)->json

Returns a JSON value parsed from the given string.

Copy

db> select to_json('[1, "hello", null]');

{Json("[1, \"hello\", null]")}

Copy

db> select to_json('{"hello": "world"}');

{Json("{\"hello\": \"world\"}")}

std::json_array_unpack(json: json)->set of json

Returns the elements of a JSON array as a set of [`json`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/json#type::std::json).

Calling this function on anything other than a JSON array will result in a runtime error.

This function should be used only if the ordering of elements is not important, or when the ordering of the set is preserved (such as an immediate input to an aggregate function).

Copy

db> select json_array_unpack(to_json('[1, "a"]'));

{Json("1"), Json("\"a\"")}

std::json_get(json: json, variadic path: str)->optional json

Returns a value from a JSON object or array given its path.

This function provides "safe" navigation of a JSON value. If the input path is a valid path for the input JSON object/array, the JSON value at the end of that path is returned:

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... select json_get(to_json('{ "q": 1, "w": [2, "foo"], "e": true }'), 'w', '1');

{Json("\"foo\"")}

This is useful when certain structure of JSON data is assumed, but cannot be reliably guaranteed. If the path cannot be followed for any reason, the empty set is returned:

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... select json_get(to_json('{ "q": 1, "w": [2, "foo"], "e": true }'), 'w', '2');

{}

If you want to supply your own default for the case where the path cannot be followed, you can do so using the [`coalesce`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/set#operator::coalesce) operator:

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... select json_get(to_json('{ "q": 1, "w": [2, "foo"], "e": true }'), 'w', '2') ?? <json>'mydefault';

{Json("\"mydefault\"")}

std::json_set(target: json, variadic path: str, named only value: optional json, named only create_if_missing: bool=true, named only empty_treatment: JsonEmpty=JsonEmpty.ReturnEmpty)->optional json

Returns an updated JSON target with a new value.

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... select json_set( to_json('{"a": 10, "b": 20}'), 'a', value := <json>true, );

{Json("{\"a\": true, \"b\": 20}")}

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... select json_set( to_json('{"a": {"b": {}}}'), 'a', 'b', 'c', value := <json>42, );

{Json("{\"a\": {\"b\": {\"c\": 42}}}")}

If _create\_if\_missing_ is set to `false`, a new path for the value won't be created:

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... select json_set( to_json('{"a": 10, "b": 20}'), 'с', value := <json>42, );

{Json("{\"a\": 10, \"b\": 20, \"с\": 42}")}

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... 
... select json_set( to_json('{"a": 10, "b": 20}'), 'с', value := <json>42, create_if_missing := false, );

{Json("{\"a\": 10, \"b\": 20}")}

The _empty\_treatment_ parameter defines the behavior of the function if an empty set is passed as _new\_value_. This parameter can take these values:

*   `ReturnEmpty`: return empty set, default

*   `ReturnTarget`: return `target` unmodified

*   `Error`: raise an `InvalidValueError`

*   `UseNull`: use a `null` JSON value

*   `DeleteKey`: delete the object key

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... select json_set( to_json('{"a": 10, "b": 20}'), 'a', value := <json>{} );

{}

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... 
... select json_set( to_json('{"a": 10, "b": 20}'), 'a', value := <json>{}, empty_treatment := JsonEmpty.ReturnTarget, );

{Json("{\"a\": 10, \"b\": 20}")}

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... 
... select json_set( to_json('{"a": 10, "b": 20}'), 'a', value := <json>{}, empty_treatment := JsonEmpty.Error, );

InvalidValueError: invalid empty JSON value

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... 
... select json_set( to_json('{"a": 10, "b": 20}'), 'a', value := <json>{}, empty_treatment := JsonEmpty.UseNull, );

{Json("{\"a\": null, \"b\": 20}")}

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... 
... select json_set( to_json('{"a": 10, "b": 20}'), 'a', value := <json>{}, empty_treatment := JsonEmpty.DeleteKey, );

{Json("{\"b\": 20}")}

std::json_object_pack(pairs: SET OF tuple<str, json>)->json

Returns the given set of key/value tuples as a JSON object.

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... select json_object_pack({ ("foo", to_json("1")), ("bar", to_json("null")), ("baz", to_json("[]")) });

{Json("{\"bar\": null, \"baz\": [], \"foo\": 1}")}

If the key/value tuples being packed have common keys, the last value for each key will make the final object.

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... select json_object_pack({ ("hello", <json>"world"), ("hello", <json>true) });

{Json("{\"hello\": true}")}

std::json_object_unpack(json: json)->set of tuple<str, json>

Returns the data in a JSON object as a set of key/value tuples.

Calling this function on anything other than a JSON object will result in a runtime error.

Copy

db> 
... 
... 
... 
... select json_object_unpack(to_json('{ "q": 1, "w": [2, "foo"], "e": true }'));

{('e', Json("true")), ('q', Json("1")), ('w', Json("[2, \"foo\"]"))}

std::json_typeof(json: json)->str

Returns the type of the outermost JSON value as a string.

Possible return values are: `'object'`, `'array'`, `'string'`, `'number'`, `'boolean'`, or `'null'`:

Copy

db> select json_typeof(<json>2);

{'number'}

Copy

db> select json_typeof(to_json('null'));

{'null'}

Copy

db> select json_typeof(to_json('{"a": 2}'));

{'object'}

